NeoBoard is designed to run behind a reverse proxy in production:

- HTTPS terminates at the proxy; NeoBoard honors `X-Forwarded-Proto` for its
  `FORCE_HTTPS` redirect logic.
- Auth.js needs the public URL in `NEXTAUTH_URL` and correct
  `X-Forwarded-Host` / `X-Forwarded-Proto` headers to build callback URLs.
- Load balancers should probe `GET /api/health` (200/degraded = healthy,
  503 = unhealthy).

Set these env vars in every proxied deployment:

```bash
NEXTAUTH_URL=https://neoboard.example.com
FORCE_HTTPS=true   # optional — redirect stray http requests
```

## nginx

```nginx
server {
  listen 443 ssl http2;
  server_name neoboard.example.com;

  ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/neoboard.example.com/fullchain.pem;
  ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/neoboard.example.com/privkey.pem;

  # Long-lived dashboard queries: keep read timeouts above the app's
  # 30s query timeout so the proxy doesn't cut responses first.
  proxy_read_timeout 60s;

  location / {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Host              $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Host  $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
  }

  # Immutable Next.js build assets — cache aggressively at the proxy.
  location /_next/static/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;
    proxy_cache_valid 200 7d;
    add_header Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable";
  }
}

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name neoboard.example.com;
  return 301 https://$host$request_uri;
}
```

## Caddy

Caddy provisions TLS automatically and sets `X-Forwarded-*` headers by
default, so the minimal config is genuinely minimal:

```text
neoboard.example.com {
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
}
```

Optional health-based upstream check:

```text
neoboard.example.com {
  reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000 {
    health_uri      /api/health
    health_interval 30s
  }
}
```

## Traefik (Docker labels)

```yaml
services:
  neoboard:
    image: neoboard/community:latest
    environment:
      NEXTAUTH_URL: https://neoboard.example.com
    labels:
      - traefik.enable=true
      - traefik.http.routers.neoboard.rule=Host(`neoboard.example.com`)
      - traefik.http.routers.neoboard.entrypoints=websecure
      - traefik.http.routers.neoboard.tls.certresolver=letsencrypt
      - traefik.http.services.neoboard.loadbalancer.server.port=3000
      - traefik.http.services.neoboard.loadbalancer.healthcheck.path=/api/health
      - traefik.http.services.neoboard.loadbalancer.healthcheck.interval=30s
```

Traefik forwards `X-Forwarded-*` headers automatically for HTTP providers.

## Cloud load balancers

For AWS ALB / GCP HTTPS LB, the same three rules apply:

1. Target group / backend service on port `3000`.
2. Health check path `/api/health` — treat **200 as healthy**; the endpoint
   returns 503 when required config is missing or the metadata DB is
   unreachable.
3. Terminate TLS at the LB and make sure `X-Forwarded-Proto: https` reaches
   the app (both ALB and GCP LB do this by default).

## Checklist

- [ ] `NEXTAUTH_URL` matches the public HTTPS URL exactly
- [ ] `X-Forwarded-Proto` / `X-Forwarded-Host` reach the app
- [ ] Health probes hit `/api/health`
- [ ] Proxy read timeout ≥ the app's query timeout (default 30s)

NeoBoard's connector system is plugin-based. Each database type (Neo4j, PostgreSQL) is a self-contained plugin. You can add support for new databases by creating a connector plugin.

## Quick Start

Create a plugin file in the connection package:

```ts
// connection/src/my-database/plugin.ts
import type { ConnectorPlugin } from "../generalized/connector-plugin";
import type { AuthConfig } from "../generalized/interfaces";
import { MyDatabaseConnectionModule } from "./MyDatabaseConnectionModule";

export const myDatabasePlugin: ConnectorPlugin = {
  type: "my-database",
  label: "My Database",
  category: "database",
  queryLanguage: "sql",
  supportsWrite: true,
  supportsGraphData: false,
  allowedProtocols: ["mydb:", "mydb+ssl:"],
  uriPlaceholder: "mydb://localhost:5555/mydb",
  databasePlaceholder: "mydb",

  createModule(authConfig: AuthConfig, advancedOptions?: Record<string, unknown>) {
    return new MyDatabaseConnectionModule(authConfig, advancedOptions);
  },
};
```

Then register it in `connection/src/connector-registry.ts`:

```ts
import { myDatabasePlugin } from "./my-database/plugin";

// Add after the existing registrations:
registry.register(myDatabasePlugin);
```

## Connector Plugin Reference

```ts
interface ConnectorPlugin {
  // Required
  type: string;           // unique ID: "mysql", "mongodb", "rest-api"
  label: string;          // display name: "MySQL", "MongoDB"
  category: "database" | "graph" | "api" | "file";
  createModule(auth, opts): ConnectionModule;  // factory for the driver

  // Optional
  supportsGraphData?: boolean;    // can produce nodes/edges? (default: false)
  supportsWrite?: boolean;        // supports INSERT/CREATE? (default: false)
  queryLanguage?: string;         // "cypher", "sql", or custom
  allowedProtocols?: string[];    // URI validation: ["mysql:", "mysql+ssl:"]
  uriPlaceholder?: string;        // shown in connection form
  databasePlaceholder?: string;   // shown in database field
}
```

## Implementing a ConnectionModule

Your connector must extend the abstract `ConnectionModule` class:

```ts
import { ConnectionModule } from "../generalized/ConnectionModule";
import { AuthenticationModule } from "../generalized/AuthenticationModule";
import type { ConnectionConfig, QueryCallback, QueryParams } from "../generalized/interfaces";

export class MyDatabaseConnectionModule extends ConnectionModule {
  authModule: AuthenticationModule;

  constructor(authConfig: AuthConfig, advancedOptions?: Record<string, unknown>) {
    super();
    // Initialize your driver here
    this.authModule = new MyDatabaseAuthModule(authConfig);
  }

  async runQuery<T>(
    queryParams: QueryParams,
    callbacks: QueryCallback<T>,
    config: ConnectionConfig,
  ): Promise<void> {
    // Handle empty query
    if (this.handleEmptyQuery(queryParams.query, callbacks)) return;

    try {
      // Execute the query using your driver
      const results = await this.executeQuery(queryParams.query, queryParams.params);

      // Convert results to NeoBoard's record format
      const records = results.map(row => ({
        // Map each column to key-value pairs
        ...row,
      }));

      // Call the success callback
      if (callbacks.setRecords) {
        callbacks.setRecords(records as T);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      if (callbacks.setError) {
        callbacks.setError(error);
      }
    }
  }

  async checkConnection(config?: ConnectionConfig): Promise<boolean> {
    try {
      // Try to connect and run a simple query
      await this.executeQuery("SELECT 1");
      return true;
    } catch {
      return false;
    }
  }
}
```

## Built-in Connectors

NeoBoard ships with two built-in connector plugins:

### Neo4j
- **Type:** `"neo4j"`
- **Category:** `"graph"`
- **Language:** Cypher
- **Graph data:** Yes (nodes, relationships, paths)
- **Write:** Yes (CREATE, MERGE, DELETE)
- **Protocols:** `bolt:`, `neo4j:`, `bolt+s:`, `neo4j+s:`

### PostgreSQL
- **Type:** `"postgresql"`
- **Category:** `"database"`
- **Language:** SQL
- **Graph data:** No
- **Write:** Yes (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE)
- **Protocols:** `postgresql:`, `postgres:`

## Registry API

```ts
import {
  registerConnector,
  getConnector,
  getAllConnectors,
  createConnectionModule,
} from "@neoboard/connection/src/connector-registry";

// Register a new connector
registerConnector(myPlugin);

// Look up a connector
const plugin = getConnector("mysql");

// List all registered connectors
const all = getAllConnectors(); // [neo4jPlugin, postgresPlugin, ...]

// Create a connection module (replaces the old factory)
const module = createConnectionModule("neo4j", authConfig, advancedOptions);
```

## Testing Your Connector

Use the existing test patterns:

```ts
// connection/__tests__/my-database/plugin.test.ts
import { createConnectorRegistry } from "../src/generalized/connector-plugin";
import { myDatabasePlugin } from "../src/my-database/plugin";

describe("myDatabasePlugin", () => {
  it("registers without error", () => {
    const registry = createConnectorRegistry();
    expect(() => registry.register(myDatabasePlugin)).not.toThrow();
  });

  it("has correct type and category", () => {
    expect(myDatabasePlugin.type).toBe("my-database");
    expect(myDatabasePlugin.category).toBe("database");
  });

  it("creates a connection module", () => {
    const module = myDatabasePlugin.createModule({
      uri: "mydb://localhost:5555/test",
      username: "user",
      password: "pass",
      authType: 1,
    });
    expect(module).toBeDefined();
    expect(typeof module.runQuery).toBe("function");
    expect(typeof module.checkConnection).toBe("function");
  });
});
```

## Error Handling

NeoBoard provides standard error types for connectors. Use these in your `ConnectionModule` so the UI can show appropriate messages:

```ts
import {
  ConnectionError,
  QueryError,
  QueryTimeoutError,
  SchemaError,
} from "@neoboard/connection";
```

| Error Type | When to Throw | UI Behavior |
|------------|---------------|-------------|
| `ConnectionError` | Can't connect (credentials, host, SSL) | Shows connection settings hint |
| `QueryError` | Query failed (syntax, permissions) | Shows error in widget card |
| `QueryTimeoutError` | Query exceeded timeout | Shows retry option |
| `SchemaError` | Schema introspection failed | Disables autocomplete |

## Registration Validation

NeoBoard validates your plugin on registration:

- **Required fields**: `type`, `label`, `createModule` — throws if missing
- **formFields**: warns on missing key/label/type, duplicate keys, select fields with no options
- **category**: warns if not one of `database`, `graph`, `api`, `file`

This guide walks through adding a new chart type to NeoBoard, from registration to rendering.

## Overview

Adding a chart type involves:
1. Define the chart type identifier
2. Create the chart component in `component/`
3. Register a transform function in the chart registry
4. Add a data validator
5. Add to the chart type picker UI
6. Write tests

## Step 1: Define the Chart Type

Add your chart type to the `ChartType` union in `component/src/charts/types.ts`:

```typescript
export type ChartType =
  | "bar"
  | "line"
  | "pie"
  | "single-value"
  | "table"
  | "graph"
  | "map"
  | "json"
  | "your-new-chart"; // Add here
```

## Step 2: Create the Chart Component

Create a new file in `component/src/charts/`:

```typescript
// component/src/charts/your-new-chart.tsx
import type { BaseChartProps } from "./types";

interface YourNewChartProps extends BaseChartProps {
  // Add chart-specific props
}

export function YourNewChart({ options, loading, error, className }: YourNewChartProps) {
  // Use ECharts BaseChart or build a custom renderer
  // ...
}
```

**Important**: Use modular ECharts imports only:
```typescript
import * as echarts from "echarts/core";
import { BarChart } from "echarts/charts";
// Never: import * as echarts from "echarts"
```

## Step 3: Register in Chart Registry

Add your chart to `app/src/lib/chart-registry.ts`:

```typescript
registry.set("your-new-chart", {
  label: "Your New Chart",
  icon: ChartIcon, // from lucide-react
  transform: (data, settings) => {
    // Transform query results into ECharts options
    return { /* EChartsOption */ };
  },
  validate: (data) => {
    // Return true if data shape is compatible
    return data.length > 0 && data[0].hasOwnProperty("value");
  },
  defaultSettings: {
    // Default chart options
  },
});
```

## Step 4: Add to Chart Type Picker

Update the widget editor to include your chart in the type selection dropdown.

## Step 5: Dynamic Import

Charts must be lazy-loaded with SSR disabled:

```typescript
// In the page renderer
const YourNewChart = dynamic(
  () => import("@neoboard/components").then((m) => m.YourNewChart),
  { ssr: false }
);
```

## Step 6: Write Tests

1. **Chart registry test** — verify transform and validate functions
2. **Component test** — render the chart with mock data (in `component/`)
3. **E2E test** — create a widget with the new chart type

NeoBoard's chart system is plugin-based. Each chart type (bar, line, pie, etc.) is a self-contained plugin that registers itself with the chart registry. You can add new chart types by creating a single plugin file.

## Quick Start

Create a file at `app/src/plugins/your-chart.tsx`:

```tsx
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";
import { Skeleton } from "@neoboard/components";
import { defineChartPlugin } from "./registry";
import { useEChartsClick, type PluginProps } from "./utils";

// Lazy-load your chart component (ECharts needs client-side only)
const MyChart = dynamic(
  () => import("@neoboard/components").then((m) => ({ default: m.MyChart })),
  { ssr: false, loading: () => <Skeleton className="w-full h-full" /> },
);

function MyChartPlugin({ data, settings, onChartClick }: PluginProps) {
  const onClick = useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data);
  return (
    <MyChart
      data={data ?? []}
      showLegend={settings.showLegend as boolean}
      onClick={onClick}
    />
  );
}

export const myChartPlugin = defineChartPlugin({
  type: "my-chart",
  label: "My Chart",
  component: MyChartPlugin,
  transform: (rows) => rows, // transform raw query rows to chart data
  compatibleWith: ["neo4j", "postgresql"],
  capabilities: {
    supportsClickAction: true,
    supportsStyling: false,
    isECharts: true,
    requiresQuery: true,
  },
  queryHint: "Return label and value columns.\nExample: RETURN name, count",
  options: [
    {
      key: "showLegend",
      label: "Show Legend",
      type: "boolean",
      default: true,
      category: "Labels",
      description: "Show the chart legend.",
    },
  ],
});
```

Then register it in `app/src/plugins/index.ts`:

```ts
import { myChartPlugin } from "./my-chart";

// Add to the BUILT_IN_PLUGINS array:
const BUILT_IN_PLUGINS = [
  // ... existing plugins
  myChartPlugin,
];
```

That's it. Your chart appears in the widget editor's chart type picker.

## Plugin Config Reference

```ts
defineChartPlugin({
  // Required
  type: string,           // unique ID: "heatmap", "funnel", etc.
  label: string,          // display name in the UI
  component: React.FC,    // React component that renders the chart
  transform: (data) => T, // convert raw query rows to chart's data shape

  // Optional
  transformWithMapping?: (data, mapping) => T,  // with column mapping
  validate?: (data) => string | null,           // return error or null
  options?: ChartOptionDef[],                   // chart options panel entries
  queryHint?: string,                           // shown in query editor
  compatibleWith?: ConnectorType[],             // ["neo4j", "postgresql"]
  stylingTargets?: { value, label }[],          // enables rule-based styling
  enrichClickEvent?: (event, row) => event,     // customize click payload

  // Capabilities (all have sensible defaults)
  capabilities?: {
    supportsClickAction: boolean,  // default: true
    supportsStyling: boolean,      // default: true if stylingTargets set
    isECharts: boolean,            // default: false
    requiresQuery: boolean,        // default: true
  },
});
```

## Plugin Component Props

Every plugin component receives these props via `PluginProps` (import from `./utils`):

| Prop | Type | Description |
|------|------|-------------|
| `data` | `unknown` | Transformed query result data |
| `settings` | `Record<string, unknown>` | User-configured chart options |
| `onChartClick` | `(row) => void` | Raw click callback (use `useEChartsClick()` for ECharts) |
| `stylingRules` | `StylingRule[]` | Conditional color rules |
| `paramValues` | `Record<string, unknown>` | Current parameter values |
| `colorScales` | `ColorScaleConfig[]` | Gradient color scales |
| `connectionId` | `string` | Current connector ID |
| `widgetId` | `string` | Widget identifier |
| `resultId` | `string` | Query result cache ID |
| `query` | `string` | Current query text |
| `autoFit` | `boolean` | Auto-fit on resize |
| `colorThresholds` | `string` | Legacy threshold JSON |

## Chart Options

Options define what users can configure in the Chart Options panel:

```ts
interface ChartOptionDef {
  key: string;          // settings key (e.g. "showLegend")
  label: string;        // UI label
  type: "boolean" | "select" | "text" | "number" | "column-multi-select";
  default: unknown;     // default value
  category: string;     // group heading (e.g. "Layout", "Labels", "Style")
  options?: { label: string; value: string }[];  // for "select" type
  description?: string; // tooltip text
}
```

## ECharts vs Custom Charts

**ECharts-based charts** (bar, line, pie, gauge, etc.):
- Use `dynamic(() => import("@neoboard/components")...)` with `ssr: false`
- Use `useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data)` to wrap the click handler
- Set `capabilities.isECharts: true`

**Non-ECharts charts** (graph, map, table, form):
- Import components directly (no dynamic import needed if not using browser APIs)
- Use `onChartClick` directly (no wrapping needed)
- Set `capabilities.isECharts: false`

**Content-only widgets** (markdown, iframe):
- Set `capabilities.requiresQuery: false`
- `transform` returns `null`
- No click action or styling

## Advanced Plugin Options

The four fields below are optional but used by every built-in chart that needs more than a static transform. Source of truth: [`app/src/lib/plugin/chart-plugin-registry.ts`](https://github.com/alfredo1996/neoboard/blob/main/app/src/lib/plugin/chart-plugin-registry.ts).

### `transformWithMapping(data, mapping)`

**Signature**
```ts
transformWithMapping?: (
  data: unknown,
  mapping: ColumnMapping,   // { xAxis?: string; yAxis?: string[]; groupBy?: string }
) => unknown;
```

**When it's called.** The widget editor lets users override auto-detected axis columns via the Column Mapping overlay (Bar/Line/Pie). When a mapping exists on the widget, the registry calls `transformWithMapping(rows, mapping)` in place of `transform(rows)`. If `transformWithMapping` is not defined, mappings are silently ignored.

**Return shape.** Same shape your `transform` returns — the renderer doesn't care which path produced the data.

**Working example.** The bar plugin uses one transform for both paths:

```ts
// app/src/plugins/bar/transform.ts
export function transformToBarData(
  data: unknown,
  mapping?: ColumnMapping,
): unknown {
  const records = toRecords(data);
  if (!records.length) return [];
  const keys = Object.keys(records[0]);
  const labelKey = resolveLabelKey(keys, mapping);          // mapping.xAxis wins
  const valueKeys = resolveValueKeys(keys, labelKey, mapping); // mapping.yAxis wins
  return records.map((r) => ({
    label: String(normalizeValue(r[labelKey]) ?? ""),
    ...Object.fromEntries(valueKeys.map((k) => [k, Number(r[k]) || 0])),
  }));
}
```

The same function is registered under both keys: `transform: transformToBarData, transformWithMapping: transformToBarData`.

See: `app/src/plugins/bar/component.tsx`, `app/src/plugins/line/component.tsx`, `app/src/plugins/pie/component.tsx`.

### `validate(data)`

**Signature**
```ts
validate?: (data: unknown) => string | null;
```

**When it's called.** After `transform` runs but before the chart renders. The widget shows the returned string as an "Incompatible data" message in place of the chart. Returning `null` means the data is valid — including the empty case. An empty array should resolve to `null` so the widget renders its "No data" state, not an error.

**What the UI does with it.** The error string is shown verbatim inside the widget body — so write it as user-facing copy with at least one concrete column requirement and ideally an example query.

**Working example.**

```ts
// app/src/plugins/bar/transform.ts
export function validateBarData(data: unknown): string | null {
  const records = toRecords(data);
  if (!records.length) return null; // empty -> "No data" state, not an error
  const cols = Object.keys(records[0]).length;
  if (cols < 2) {
    return `Bar chart requires at least 2 columns: first for labels, one or more for values. Your query returned ${cols} column(s). Example: SELECT category, count FROM ...`;
  }
  return null;
}
```

See: every chart plugin (`pie`, `line`, `gauge`, `sankey`, …) wires a similarly shaped `validate*` helper.

### `enrichClickEvent(event, row)`

**Signature**
```ts
enrichClickEvent?: (
  event: Record<string, unknown>,
  row: Record<string, unknown>,
) => Record<string, unknown>;
```

**When it's called.** Right before a click action fires — the registry hands the raw ECharts event and the original data row to the plugin, lets the plugin attach chart-specific fields, then dispatches the enriched event to click-action handlers (set-parameter, navigate page) and to rule-based styling resolution.

**Why it exists.** Some chart types collapse the source row before rendering (e.g. pie aggregates by category), so the click handler would otherwise lose access to the underlying field. Enriching the event re-attaches those fields under stable names that click-action templates and styling rules can reference.

**Currently used by.** None of the built-in plugins need this yet — the default behavior of forwarding `row` as-is is enough for every shipping chart. The hook is provided for plugin authors whose chart aggregates or re-keys data before rendering. Recipe:

```ts
enrichClickEvent: (event, row) => ({
  ...event,
  // Surface the original aggregation key under a stable name the click-action
  // template can reference, e.g. {{event.bucketKey}}.
  bucketKey: row.bucket,
  rawCount: row.count,
}),
```

### `stylingTargets`

**Signature**
```ts
stylingTargets?: { value: string; label: string }[];
```

**What it controls.** Rule-based styling lets users set values like color or font weight based on data conditions (e.g. "if value > 100, color = red"). `stylingTargets` is the list of *what* a rule can set on this chart. Each entry becomes an option in the rule editor's "Target" dropdown.

**How it maps to the chart-options-panel.** Targets are surfaced in the Advanced → Conditional Styling section of the Chart Settings panel (`component/src/components/composed/chart-settings-panel.tsx`). The plugin component is responsible for reading resolved rules from its `stylingRules` prop and applying the matched target value at render time.

**Capabilities side-effect.** When `stylingTargets` is non-empty, `defineChartPlugin` flips `capabilities.supportsStyling` to `true` automatically. You only need to set the capability explicitly when you want to *disable* styling on a chart that does have targets (rare).

**Working examples.**

```ts
// Single target — most common
stylingTargets: [{ value: "color", label: "Bar Color" }],         // bar
stylingTargets: [{ value: "color", label: "Line Color" }],        // line

// Multi-target — value cell vs. background
stylingTargets: [
  { value: "valueColor",      label: "Value Color" },
  { value: "backgroundColor", label: "Background Color" },
],                                                                // single-value
```

See: `app/src/plugins/bar/component.tsx`, `app/src/plugins/single-value/component.tsx`, `app/src/plugins/table/component.tsx`.

## Example: Adding a Heatmap

```tsx
// app/src/plugins/heatmap.tsx
import dynamic from "next/dynamic";
import { Skeleton } from "@neoboard/components";
import { defineChartPlugin } from "./registry";
import { useEChartsClick, type PluginProps } from "./utils";

const HeatmapChart = dynamic(
  () => import("../components/charts/heatmap-chart").then((m) => ({ default: m.HeatmapChart })),
  { ssr: false, loading: () => <Skeleton className="w-full h-full" /> },
);

function HeatmapPlugin({ data, settings, onChartClick }: PluginProps) {
  const onClick = useEChartsClick(onChartClick, data);
  return (
    <HeatmapChart
      data={data ?? []}
      xField={settings.xField as string}
      yField={settings.yField as string}
      valueField={settings.valueField as string}
      onClick={onClick}
    />
  );
}

export const heatmapPlugin = defineChartPlugin({
  type: "heatmap",
  label: "Heatmap",
  component: HeatmapPlugin,
  transform: (rows) => rows,
  compatibleWith: ["neo4j", "postgresql"],
  capabilities: { supportsClickAction: true, isECharts: true, requiresQuery: true, supportsStyling: false },
  queryHint: "Return 3 columns: x, y, value.\nExample: RETURN day, hour, count",
  options: [
    { key: "xField", label: "X Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
    { key: "yField", label: "Y Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
    { key: "valueField", label: "Value Field", type: "text", default: "", category: "Data" },
  ],
});
```

This guide covers implementing a new database connector in the `connection/` package.

## Overview

A connector consists of four classes plus a factory registration:
1. **AuthenticationModule** — manages connection pool and credentials
2. **ConnectionModule** — executes queries and tests connections
3. **RecordParser** — transforms raw results into NeoBoard's format
4. **SchemaManager** (optional) — introspects database schema

New files needed: 4 (auth module, connection module, record parser, advanced options type).
Existing files to edit: 2 (factory registration + `ConnectionModuleConfig` enum).

## Step 1: Add Connection Type

In `connection/src/ConnectionModuleConfig.ts`:

```typescript
export enum ConnectionTypes {
  NEO4J = 1,
  POSTGRESQL = 2,
  YOUR_DB = 3, // Add here
}
```

## Step 2: Define Advanced Options

Each connector has its own advanced options type. Add yours in `connection/src/generalized/interfaces.ts`:

```typescript
export interface YourDbAdvancedOptions extends BaseAdvancedOptions {
  yourDbConnectionTimeout?: number;
  yourDbMaxPoolSize?: number;
}
```

Then add it to the union type:

```typescript
export type AdvancedConnectionOptions =
  | Neo4jAdvancedOptions
  | PostgresAdvancedOptions
  | YourDbAdvancedOptions;
```

## Step 3: Implement AuthenticationModule

Extend the abstract `AuthenticationModule`. The `createDriver()` method returns `unknown` — your subclass narrows to the concrete driver type.

```typescript
// connection/src/yourdb/YourDbAuthenticationModule.ts
